<h2>Caruso Kitchens: A Dealer Doing It Right</h2>
<p><a href="https://carusokitchens.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caruso Kitchens</a> has built a reputation for bringing thoughtful, design-forward cabinetry to their market — and their <a href="/brands/eclipse-cabinetry">Eclipse Cabinetry</a> showroom is a perfect example of how to merchandise a product line the right way. Clean vignettes, real materials, smart finish pairings, and a design story that homeowners can actually walk through and feel.</p>
<p>What you'll see in the video: a mix of Eclipse door styles and finishes staged the way they'd live in a client's home — not the way a spec sheet lays them out. That's the difference between a showroom that sells and a showroom that just displays product.</p>

<h2>Dealers: Producing Content Is Now Part of the Job</h2>
<p>The dealers growing fastest in 2026 have one thing in common — they're publishing their own content. Not manufacturer stock photos. Not brochure shots. <em>Their own showroom, on camera, in their own voice.</em></p>
<p>Why this matters more every quarter:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The kitchen buying journey starts on a phone.</strong> Before a homeowner books a consultation, they've already scrolled Instagram, Pinterest, TikTok, and YouTube for hours. If your work isn't there, you're invisible at the top of the funnel.</li>
  <li><strong>Short-form video beats every other medium for trust.</strong> A 45-second walkthrough of a real showroom earns more credibility than a month of static posts. People buy from people — and video shows the people.</li>
  <li><strong>Content is an appreciating asset.</strong> A single well-produced video keeps generating leads, referrals, and designer inquiries long after it's published. Static ads stop working the day your budget runs out.</li>
  <li><strong>You become the authority in your market.</strong> The dealer who explains Eclipse's construction on camera becomes "the Eclipse expert" in their region. Referrals follow authority.</li>
</ul>

